    "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term",
    "problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-4y+10.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}+6.\n$$",
